#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087453 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbf50e27-a8c3-52e7-be21-5d7ed61e5512" :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Table of Values for a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "53ce2176-ae44-578d-8fba-35c5018d4162" :question/math-academy-id "e-5075" :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n$x=3t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -3\\leq t \\leq 3$,\n\nfill in the following table of values.\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=3t$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$-values using $y=t^3$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $y$ | $-27$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ | $27$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087457 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc11d5-8eda-52b1-b7b1-42b481813ff4" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/id #uuid "b322617c-ab21-5ce8-997f-2f10813b4280" :question/math-academy-id "e-5098" :question/problem "Plot the graph of the parametrically defined curve\n$x=t(t-6), \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -2\\leq t \\leq 2$." :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-6)$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t^3$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/803/Source/Images/e492a940fa95b406b60c6b0a5c438d98.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087458 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbf0ad0-a7c5-5c0f-a06c-9210fdc244ef" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "a792fa16-e1fd-5f30-a128-bbc49a285429" :question/math-academy-id "e-5099" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n$x=t^2-6t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^4+4, \\quad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$,\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x$, we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}x & = {t}^{2} - 6 t \\\\  & = {( t - 3 )}^{2} - ( 3 {)}^{2} \\\\  & = {( t - 3 )}^{2} - 9\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSince $\\left(t-3\\right)^2 \\geq 0$, we can conclude that $x \\geq -9$.\n- To find the range of $y=t^4+4$, notice that $t^4 \\geq 0$, and consequently $y \\geq 4$.\n\nTherefore, the ranges are\n\n$$\nx \\geq -9\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny \\geq 4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbf50e27-a8c3-52e7-be21-5d7ed61e5512" :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Table of Values for a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f95577b-632c-54e4-8706-1573c2bccfd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263651} {:answer/id #uuid "ff734e88-a8ee-59cd-85d1-b7fb1f2fbab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263652} {:answer/id #uuid "87f46e39-8e8a-5834-ada2-411db6ec5148" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263653} {:answer/id #uuid "6102984e-143f-5cdd-8c5e-1981df040390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263654} {:answer/id #uuid "f68ccb7f-0ca3-58e3-bf8b-e725d4fcdac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f68ccb7f-0ca3-58e3-bf8b-e725d4fcdac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263655} :answer-field/id #uuid "414ccbaf-6ec3-577f-b24f-316abfaa7510" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263650}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2e40174-9d6b-571b-bd04-23bcce514887" :question/math-academy-id "q-123753" :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=1-{t}^{2},\\,\\,\\,y=3t+2,\\,-2\\le t\\le 2,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $a$ | $1$ | $0$ | $c$ |\n| $y$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $b$ | $5$ | $8$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=-1$ into $x=1-{t}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=1-(-1)^{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=0$ into $y=3t+2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=3(0)+2=2.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=2$ into $x=1-{t}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=1-(2)^{2}=-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0+2+(-3)=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbf0ad0-a7c5-5c0f-a06c-9210fdc244ef" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e2e46fa-845d-5d2a-9713-4edf129f2a99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y<-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263658} {:answer/id #uuid "18400921-7a23-5e3d-a8b5-b49660d0f54c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263659} {:answer/id #uuid "b017aae2-e626-5e99-a5a0-31b4b61e867a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263660} {:answer/id #uuid "d25af62e-d94f-55c4-a74f-322857e39e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y>-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263661} {:answer/id #uuid "40192662-5865-5218-9c2e-334a4e93b7ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b017aae2-e626-5e99-a5a0-31b4b61e867a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263660} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0c409ee-32bd-5c3e-943c-2a7ccfbff6cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263657}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12184c96-fdfc-58db-88c7-0f863d4011b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-123767" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t},\\,\\,\\,y=1-t,\\,t>0,\n$$\n\nwhat is the range of $y?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the range of $y,$ note that $y=1-t<1$ for all $t>0.$ \n\nConsequently, the range of $y$ is $y<1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc11d5-8eda-52b1-b7b1-42b481813ff4" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be14d99c-b68e-5ee4-923b-68e4f3ba0c24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13948638/assets/16e0429bb738f3cdee4f20a363ecdae6457ba18526c159c20ab69244f0bf6a2e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263665} {:answer/id 